.TH pdfregions 1 "10 November 2026"
.SH NAME
pdfregions \- render regions of PDF pages as RGB pixels or SVG
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pdfregions
[options]
.I PDF-file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pdfregions
reads render jobs from standard input, one per line, and writes one record
per job to standard output, in job order. It writes no files and makes no
decisions about what to extract; it is the renderer behind
.B pdftomarkdown \-\-images\-dir
and implements docslurp/extraction/PROTOCOL.md.
.PP
Regions are given in the coordinate space of
.B pdftotext \-bbox\-layout
word coordinates: points, y pointing down, page rotation applied, media box
(the crop box is never used). A box taken from
.B pdftotext \-images
output therefore frames exactly that figure.
.SH JOBS
Each line is ASCII, tab-separated and ends in a newline:
.PP
.RS
.I id page x0 y0 x1 y1 mode dpi
.RE
.TP
.I id
.B p
followed by 1 to 9 digits, a dot, an optional
.BR g ,
and 1 to 6 digits (for example
.B p3.1
or
.BR p3.g2 );
echoed back verbatim.
.TP
.I page
1-based page number. Jobs must be sorted by page (non-decreasing).
.TP
.IR "x0 y0 x1 y1"
Plain decimals (an optional minus sign, 1 to 9 integer digits and an optional
fraction of 1 to 9 digits; no exponent) with
.I x1
>
.I x0
and
.I y1
>
.IR y0 .
.TP
.I mode
.B raster
(Splash, RGB, composited on white),
.B svg
(cairo SVG 1.2, unit pt) or
.B copy
(an image XObject's own pixels, see COPY JOBS).
.TP
.I dpi
36 to 600 for
.BR raster ;
any plain decimal for
.B svg
(ignored). For
.B copy
the image's reference instead:
.IB num . gen
(num 1 to 9 digits without a leading zero, gen 0 to 65535), as
.B pdftotext \-images
writes ref="num gen".
.PP
At most 5000 jobs and 256 bytes per line. Every line is checked before
anything is rendered: a malformed line, unsorted pages, too many jobs or a
missing final newline exit with status 2 and nothing on standard output.
.SH RECORDS
.RS
.I id status kind width height bytes
.RE
.PP
tab-separated and ended by a newline, followed by exactly
.I bytes
bytes of payload.
.I status
is
.B ok
or
.BI err: code
(then
.I kind
is
.BR \- ,
and width, height and bytes are 0). Codes:
.B pixels
(the crop exceeds
.BR \-max\-pixels ),
.B render
(the renderer failed),
.B empty
(the region lies outside the page),
.B page
(no such page),
.B svgsize
(the SVG exceeds
.BR \-max\-svg\-bytes ),
and for copy jobs
.B copy
(not an image the page names, or no copy class),
.B copysize
(over
.BR \-max\-copy\-bytes ),
.B jpeg
(not a JPEG to copy) and
.B decode
(cannot decode).
.PP
A region overhanging the page is first cut to the page. A
.B raster
crop starts at device pixel
.RB floor( x0 \(mu dpi/72)
and is
.RB max(1,\ round(( x1 \- x0 )\ \(mu dpi/72))
pixels wide (likewise for y, both kept on the page), the same pixels as
.B pdftoppm \-r
.I dpi
.B \-x \-y \-W \-H
with those numbers;
.I kind
is
.B rgb8
and the payload is width \(mu height \(mu 3 bytes, rows top first.
An
.B svg
record has
.I kind
.BR svg ,
width and height 0, and a UTF-8 SVG payload whose root has
.I width
and
.I height
in pt and
.I viewBox
equal to the (cut) region, so the SVG's user space is the page's word space;
content outside the region is clipped and dropped by cairo. Embedded images
are always data:image/png (decoded pixels), never the document's own JPEG,
JPX, JBIG2 or CCITT bytes.
.PP
The document and each page are opened once and fonts are cached across jobs;
each job then draws the page clipped to its region (Splash for
.BR raster ,
so crops are pixel-identical to
.BR pdftoppm ;
cairo for
.BR svg ).
.SH COPY JOBS
A
.B copy
job answers only for an image XObject named in that page's /XObject resources,
directly or through form XObjects nested at most 4 deep (at most 10,000 entries
searched), whose dictionary still has the copy class
.B pdftotext \-images
reports as copy=. Width and height are the image's /Width and /Height.
.I kind
.B jpeg
(copy=jpeg) is the stored DCT stream once its SOI, its single SOF0, SOF1 or
SOF2 frame (8-bit, the image's size and component count), its scans and its
final EOI are checked, rewritten without metadata: every APPn segment (Exif with
its orientation and thumbnail, ICC, XMP) and every COM is dropped, a JFIF APP0
and an Adobe APP14 are kept in canonical form (they decide how the samples
decode); nothing is decoded. Its tables and scans must be ones a decoder accepts
(quantisation and Huffman table ids, Huffman counts that form a prefix code,
scan components of the frame, the spectral range of a sequential or progressive
scan), or the job is
.BR err:jpeg .
A stream without metadata comes back byte for byte.
.I kind
.B gray1
(copy=bilevel) is the decoded 1-bit samples, ceil(width/8) bytes a row, 0
black.
.I kind
.B gray8
or
.B rgb8
(copy=decode) is the samples decoded once at native size through the colour
space. Decoded copies are capped by
.BR \-max\-copy\-pixels
(8 times as many pixels for gray1). Before anything is decoded, the size the codec
would really decode must be the image's: a JPEG 2000 codestream's SIZ (raw or in a
JP2 jp2c box), a JBIG2 page information segment, CCITT /Columns and /Rows;
otherwise
.BR err:pixels .
Every segment header of a JBIG2 stream and of its JBIG2Globals is read: a region
segment larger than the page, a pattern dictionary whose bitmaps exceed the
page's pixels, a segment of unknown length or a header cut short is
.BR err:decode .
A codec under another filter is
.BR err:decode .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-opw " password"
Owner password (for encrypted files).
.TP
.BI \-upw " password"
User password (for encrypted files).
.TP
.BI \-max\-memory " MiB"
Address-space limit for the process (POSIX), 0 for none (default 2048).
Running out of memory exits with status 3.
.TP
.BI \-max\-pixels " number"
Largest raster crop, in pixels, checked before rendering (default
16777216, 4096 \(mu 4096). Larger crops answer
.BR err:pixels .
.TP
.BI \-timeout " seconds"
Wall-clock limit for the whole run, 0 for none (default 120). When it
expires the tool exits with status 3; every record already written is
complete.
.TP
.BI \-max\-svg\-bytes " number"
Largest SVG record (default 33554432); larger answers
.BR err:svgsize .
.TP
.BI \-max\-output\-bytes " number"
Total bytes written to standard output (default 536870912). A record that would
pass it is not written and the run exits with status 3.
.TP
.BI \-max\-copy\-bytes " number"
Largest JPEG a copy job returns (default 67108864); larger answers
.BR err:copysize .
.TP
.BI \-max\-copy\-pixels " number"
Largest image a copy job decodes, in pixels (default 16777216, the raster cap;
8 times that for 1-bit images); larger answers
.BR err:pixels .
.TP
.B \-no\-images
Draw no images; image data is never decoded (vector-only renders).
.TP
.BI \-confine " require|auto|off"
After opening the PDF and loading fontconfig, confine the process (Linux):
PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, a Landlock ruleset denying every filesystem write, create,
remove, rename and truncate (reads stay allowed, for fonts) and TCP, and a
seccomp filter denying execve, socket, ptrace, process_vm_readv/writev and
opening files for writing. One line
.B "pdfregions: confined=0|1 landlock=ABI seccomp=0|1"
goes to standard error.
.B auto
(default) confines when the kernel allows it,
.B require
exits with status 5 when it cannot, and
.B off
skips it. Production runs should use
.BR require .
.TP
.BI \-confine\-check " path"
Test aid: after confining, try to create
.IR path ,
open a socket and run /bin/true (only when confined), and report each as
allowed or denied on standard error.
.TP
.B \-v
Print copyright and version information.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage information.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostics on standard error are fixed strings and never include document
data. Poppler's and cairo's own messages are suppressed (on POSIX, file
descriptor 2 points at /dev/null while documents are processed).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Every job answered (some may be errors).
.TP
1
The document cannot be opened or decrypted.
.TP
2
Bad usage or a malformed job line.
.TP
3
A limit was hit (memory, timeout, output bytes); records already written stay
valid. If the timeout expires while a record is being written, that record gets
2 more seconds; a write still blocked then is cut short, so a reader must drop a
truncated last record.
.TP
4
Standard output cannot be written.
.TP
5
.B \-confine require
could not confine the process.
.PP
Any other status, or death by a signal (an allocation failure inside
Poppler's C allocator aborts), is a failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pdftotext (1),
.BR pdftoppm (1),
.BR pdftocairo (1)
